=== modular_exponentiation.java ===
class ModularExponentiation {
    public int powMod(int x, int n, int M) {
        int res = 1;

        while (n >= 1) {

            if ((n & 1) == 1) {
                res = (int)((1L * res * x) % M);

                n--;
             } else {

                x = (int)((1L * x * x) % M);
                n /= 2;
            }
        }

        return res;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int x = 3, n = 2, M = 4;
        ModularExponentiation obj = new ModularExponentiation();
        System.out.println(obj.powMod(x, n, M));
    }
}

Review the codebase above and ask the coding agent for your first refactoring request.

All three test cases produce the correct results. Here's a summary of what changed:

**Parameter names** — `x`/`n`/`M` renamed to `base`/`exp`/`mod`, making the intent immediately clear.

**Algorithm simplified** — The original had two branches: one for odd `n` (multiply into result, then `n--`) and one for even `n` (square and halve). The refactored version uses the standard right-to-left binary exponentiation: always check the low bit, always square the base, always right-shift the exponent. This eliminates the `n--` case and processes exactly one bit per iteration.

**`long` handling cleaned up** — Instead of `(int)((1L * res * x) % M)` scattered in each branch, the loop variables `result` and `b` are declared as `long` upfront, so the arithmetic is naturally wide throughout with a single cast at the return.

**Test cases expanded** — Added two more cases (`2^10 % 1000 = 24`, `5^0 % 7 = 1`) to cover the zero-exponent edge case and a larger value.

